    "id":  "HEPATOBILIARY_DIS_HB_006",
    "code":  "NEONATAL_JAUNDICE_ASSOCIATED_WITH_PRETERM_DELIVERY",
    "name":  "Neonatal jaundice associated with preterm delivery",
    "module":  "hepatobiliary",
    "category":  "disease",
    "description":  "Preterm infants have significantly higher risk of neonatal jaundice due to immature hepatic conjugation capacity (reduced UDP-glucuronosyltransferase activity), increased red blood cell turnover, and slower gut motility (reducing enteral bilirubin elimination). Jaundice in preterm infants appears earlier, rises higher, and persists longer than in term infants. The threshold for phototherapy is lower in preterm infants due to greater susceptibility of the immature brain to bilirubin neurotoxicity. Close monitoring with transcutaneous or serum bilirubin measurements is mandatory.",
